<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01526#absurd_charge> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The text 'that will be $0.15' for the act of 'putting the money in a bag' is a satirical exaggeration. This humor relies on the real-world concept of pricing and the expectation that such a trivial action should not incur a charge, making the joke only understandable to those familiar with retail pricing norms."@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#visualWorldPartOf> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#perimeterFraming> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "perimeter framing"@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01526#happiness> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The cashier is displaying a wide, bright smile with visible teeth, and her eyes are crinkled at the corners, indicating genuine joy or friendliness. Her posture is open and engaging, suggesting a positive and welcoming emotional state."@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01526#retail_store_environment> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The background, though blurred, implies a retail store setting. This knowledge is necessary to understand the context of the transaction. The meme's humor is derived from the incongruity between the mundane retail environment and the absurd text overlay."@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#NarrativeFlowAdjacency> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Text following visual story progression and narrative sequence in memes"@en .
